     1	# Integrated design and copy acceptance
     2	
     3	Load at section planning, the Step 4/5 grayscale review and the Step 8 pre-handoff check. This is the rulebook for `tools/pre-handoff-check.mjs`, not an optional separate review. The machine thresholds are in `runtime/quality-checks.json`; semantic verdicts and evidence live in the same `handoff.json` used by the checker. A missing required manual check fails acceptance. Automated detectors identify measurable patterns; a human/independent critic checks meaning and visual exceptions. Neither is an authorship detector. Impeccable findings are advisory: its `pass` means the pinned detector ran and produced parseable output, not that it found no design problems. The gate separately requires the scoped critic/manual PASS records for generic heroes, rounded-card grids, dashboard tropes, decoration and other semantic criteria; absent or failed records block handoff.
     4	
     5	## Measurement contract
     6	
     7	The reference viewport is 1440 × 900 CSS pixels at device scale one, with a full-page screenshot as well. Visit every declared screen and applicable state. Wait for local fonts and assets; log failed or blocked loads. Keep external requests, service workers and WebSockets blocked during the local check. Capture file hashes and the version under test. A reference PNG is 1440 pixels wide and named `screen--state--locale--vNN.png`; its hash identifies the version. A screenshot from another revision cannot support a PASS. The checker also renders every declared `settings.desktopWidths` width and 320 CSS px (the 400% reflow equivalent of a 1280 px desktop) and fails horizontal overflow, text that actually overflows a hidden or clipped box (ellipsis styling alone is not a failure; `body` is screened too, so `overflow-x: hidden` or `clip` on `html` and `body` cannot hide a wide layout), text or media pushed past the left edge (except inside a horizontal scroller, visually hidden sr-only content of at most 1×1 px or `clip: rect(0 0 0 0)` / `clip-path: inset(50%)`, and a focusable off-screen skip link that appears on focus) and a language/direction mismatch; this is a screen, not mobile approval, and manual zoom and text-spacing evidence stays required.
     8	
     9	Mark visible text wrappers with `data-copy-slot="ID"` and match every displayed string to the approved locale variant in the copy deck, including interface and error labels. Unlisted or mismatched text blocks final handoff. Declare semantic section, component, copy-slot and media IDs in the reference DOM and match them to the handoff inventory. The critic inspects the full screenshot and semantic outline so unmarked containers cannot evade a count. CSS/computed-style counts are recorded as measurements. Layout intent, claims, tasteful restraint and the purpose of an image remain reviewed judgments with an artifact, reviewer, explanation and brief line references. A guessed PASS flag is not evidence.
    10	
    11	Where the browser test uses a proxy, label it as a proxy in `evidence.json`. In particular, DOM element rectangles do not measure the actual colored pixels of text, images, gradients or overlapping regions. For accent coverage, the documented acceptance measure is visible accent area divided by total rendered area, separately for the full page and each reviewed viewport. The reviewer supplies an annotated color mask or measured screenshot regions for image/canvas/SVG content and any disputed DOM estimate. Use union area, not summed overlapping boxes. Borderline or unmeasurable coverage remains FAIL pending evidence; do not round 10.1% down to 10%. The automated accent screen fails closed: if a declared accent token (`--accent`, `--colors-accent`, `--colors-primary`) is not a valid colour, or no accent token is declared, the ratios are null and the handoff check fails; it is never treated as zero accent.
    12	
    13	The automated probes and axe run in a separate browser world (`tools/probe-world.mjs`, a CDP isolated world; Chromium only): they see the page's DOM and CSS but not its JavaScript globals, so a page script cannot patch `getComputedStyle`, `querySelectorAll` or `axe` to hide a failure. `tools/integrity.mjs` cross-checks the positional page: the same probes in the page's own world (a difference is a tampering sign), the page world's probe functions (they must still be native), and a JavaScript-off pass. More glass or a failing 320 px screen with JavaScript off fails as `integrity mismatch` unless the handoff's `jsOffDifferences` records the expected difference with a reason. Text contrast is cross-checked with JavaScript off too: CSS opacity and `filter: opacity()` on the text and its ancestors are composited; any other filter, a blend mode, a backdrop filter or a mask on the chain cannot be resolved, so that text is counted in `contrastEffects` and the cross-check is INCOMPLETE, never a silent pass (the glass hard fail is unchanged). Native control text (input and textarea values or placeholders, button-type input labels, a select's chosen option) is contrast-checked, and the content comparison records each control, link, image and labelled element with its accessible name and displayed value, so a label, value or `aria-label` that only script supplies makes the JavaScript-off content differ.
    14	
    15	Unresolved compositing on displayed text in either rendering blocks handoff as INCOMPLETE. Token contrast proofs and `jsOffDifferences` cannot waive it. Retaining the effect requires a reviewed measurement implementation that resolves its actual composition for every applicable rendering. Backdrop-filter remains a hard FAIL.
    16	
    17	Rendered-visibility backstop: the CSS contrast computation is the first pass; what the browser actually painted is the backstop, in both renderings (JavaScript on and off). Pixels decide visibility, not AA: the backstop asks whether the text is actually painted, uncovered and readable at all; AA (4.5:1, 3:1 for large text) stays with the CSS pass whenever the CSS pass resolves the colours, and with axe and the manual WCAG review. Targets are every rendered text node (display:contents wrappers included), every native control showing text (value, placeholder or chosen option) and every named element without painted text of its own (accessible names only, hit test only). Text is measured line by line: each text node's word ranges are grouped into line boxes, so one hidden line of a paragraph is found. The scan is directed at targets, not fixed bands: after a first look at the top of the page, every line not yet measured is scrolled into view (its scrollable ancestors, such as an app shell's main panel or a tab strip, first; then the document) and measured once finite animations have finished and the painted state of every element (opacity, transform, filter, clip-path, colours, position) is unchanged about 100 ms apart, so CSS and script-driven entrances and scroll reveals are measured at rest (bounded at 2 s per step; elements under infinite animations are left out). The page bottom is then collected again, and so is the end of every inner scrollport (an app shell's panel), so content added while scrolling is found, for at most four passes; a scan that stops at its step bound (300) or still finds new content after the last pass is INCOMPLETE. (a) A hit test at three points per line checks that the text, its own subtree or an ancestor is on top. An opaque cover (a replaced element other than an SVG, or a background colour at 90% or more opacity) at more than half the points obscures the line; a translucent cover (an SVG, which is mostly transparent shapes, a lighter background colour, a background image or gradient, a backdrop filter, other text) is compositing the CSS pass cannot see, so that line is graded by its pixels. Layers a hit test cannot see are possible covers too: a positioned `::before` or `::after` that paints, on the text's element or an ancestor (a loading veil, a scrim), and a positioned `pointer-events:none` layer that paints; a line under one at more than half the points is graded by its pixels at 3:1. Layers behind the text (negative z-index) are not covers. A gradient pseudo-element is the fade of a truncated block (a "read more" clamp) only when its ancestor clips text that actually overflows and the gradient sits at the edge where the text is cut; the lines under it are then cut text, not displayed, like a clipped line (the width screen still fails a partly clipped block, as the clipping rule below requires). Any other gradient pseudo-element, such as a veil over a card whose text fits, is a cover graded by its pixels. On a declared state other than `default`, the top layer is respected: with a modal dialog open (or a shown element with `aria-modal="true"`) the rest of the page is inert and is counted as not displayed, page text an open popover covers is not graded, and the dialog's and the popover's own text are graded; a non-modal open dialog is not inert, so text it covers stays graded and is obscured. (b) Pixels: from a viewport screenshot after the page settles, over the middle 60% of the line box, the background is the modal colour, ink pixels differ from it by more than 24 in a channel, the readability ratio is the 90th percentile of the ink pixels' contrast against the background and the peak is the strongest ink pixel. Where the CSS pass resolved the colours and no translucent layer covers the line, the line fails only when it is effectively not painted: fewer than three ink pixels or a peak under 1.5:1 (colours that resolve to at least 3:1 but paint under 1.5:1 are hidden by an effect the CSS pass cannot see; light and thin weights that pass AA by colour are left to the CSS pass). Where the CSS pass could not resolve the colours, the pixels grade readability: under 3:1 is unreadable (under 1.5:1 for a line with fewer than 30 ink pixels, such as a single thin glyph). Rendered text under 4 px high is unreadable. Text that is obscured or unreadable with JavaScript on fails; text that is readable with JavaScript on but obscured, unreadable, unmeasurable or hidden with it off is an integrity mismatch, and a reviewed `jsOffDifferences` entry for it never makes it PASS. Text clipped to nothing by an ancestor that cannot be scrolled (an inactive carousel slide, an accordion collapsed with max-height:0), the same in both renderings, is not displayed: it is counted (`textHidden`) like display:none and graded through the declared states that show it. That text counts as not displayed only when a declared state shows it, which the width screen enforces (below). Text the backstop cannot measure (no box, outside the document, too few pixels, not reachable by scrolling, not reached before the bound) is INCOMPLETE, never PASS. Hide inactive content with `hidden`, `display:none` or `visibility:hidden`; visually hidden assistive text (the same sr-only rule as the width screen), an off-screen skip link that appears on focus, and the text of a disabled control (no contrast requirement in WCAG 1.4.3) are counted, not graded. Native authored strings (button-type input values, placeholders, option labels and accessible names) are copy: they are matched to the copy deck and screened for placeholders and filler like visible text, and a control's displayed value and its accessible name are compared separately with JavaScript off. The copy deck approves the displayed text and the accessible name separately (a slot's optional `ariaLabel`); a name identical to the displayed value is one string. A name taken by `aria-labelledby` from a hidden or undisplayed element is a name to approve the same way, and an approved `ariaLabel` must appear in its cited source like the text.
    18	
    19	Automated contrast incomplete results: axe reports `color-contrast` as incomplete when it cannot resolve the colours (text over a photo, a gradient or a scrim). Such a result is resolved only when the backstop measured that exact element (the same composed path), with every line uncovered, and its faintest text measured at or above the AA ratio for its size (4.5:1, or 3:1 for large text) in BOTH renderings, and a PASS `wcag-review` record for the same screen, language and state cites the screenshot (its artifact is that state's approved frame or the checker's screenshot, or its note names one of them). The faintest-text measure never drops a faint glyph. It captures the element's text box four times: as painted, with the text made transparent (the local background under every glyph pixel), and with the text forced to solid black and to solid white. Glyph coverage is the difference between the black and white captures, so it does not depend on the text's own contrast: a word on a patch of nearly its own colour is still measured. In every glyph-core pixel (coverage at least 75%; the anti-aliased edge is excluded) the painted text colour is recovered and graded against its own local background. Core pixels are grouped into connected clusters (a glyph or touching glyphs), each cluster counts its median pixel, and the element's ratio is the MINIMUM over all clusters: one word over the light part of a photo (white on #c8c8c8, 1.67:1) fails the whole element even when the rest of the line sits on a dark area. Small clusters with a low ratio are kept; only punctuation-size clusters (fewer than 12 core pixels) over a solid background are left out, and only while larger clusters remain. A high percentile of all ink is never used for AA. Neither is a percentile of the clusters. When the coverage cannot be measured the element is INCOMPLETE. The resolution is recorded per element (`contrastNodes`); any other incomplete rule, and any element that fails one condition, stays blocking. One exception covers the JavaScript-off rendering only: a declared non-default state reachable only by script, declared `requiresScript: true` with its trigger recorded, resolves on the scripted rendering alone (same element, AA and review conditions); the default state always needs both renderings, and a `requiresScript` claim on a state that renders with JavaScript off (every text the scripted rendering shows is shown without script, compared by text rather than element position) fails as an integrity mismatch. For a valid claim the JavaScript-off baseline is the default state's JavaScript-off rendering, and content present only in the scripted state passes only as approved copy slots placed in that state. A script-only state may replace a default slot (search results replacing the page description) only when the copy deck places the new slot in that state and does not place the old one there; a replaced slot that is still placed in the state, and any change to copy both renderings show, stay INCOMPLETE. The `requiresScript` check compares counted word tokens, so splitting a text run into inline elements does not change it.
    20	
    21	ENH backlog: a future exception for an unresolved compositing effect is acceptable only with a pixel-measured proof per rendering (screenshot text-pixel contrast with the effect applied, a named approver and the evidence hash), never with token pairs.
    22	
    23	Clipping is one rule based on states and widths, whatever the CSS pattern: it applies to text and controls only. Cropped media is never clipped content: an image cropped by its frame, an image centred in a `picture`, a hover zoom, a round avatar crop, a cropped video, a decorative SVG or shape that bleeds out of its section, `object-fit` and background images all pass; text or a control inside a cropped media wrapper is still checked, and a box that cuts nothing (or only media and decoration) is not a clip. Content Chrome does not render is not on screen: the content of a closed `<details>` (a mobile menu), `display:none`, `content-visibility: hidden` and closed popovers are not measured, and their open state is graded only when it is declared. (1) Any text or control shown at 1440 in a state must be fully shown, or reachable by scrolling, at every declared width, 320 included, in that state, or it fails (a table whose columns an `overflow:hidden` wrapper cuts at 320, nav links cut by a nowrap bar); text or a control shown at 1440 but not shown at a width (`display:none` below a breakpoint: a hidden Subscribe button or terms line) fails there too (WCAG 1.4.10, no loss of content or function) unless the same text or accessible name is reachable at that width, in the same state (a footer link) or in a declared state at that width (`menu-open` for a mobile menu); matching is by text or name, never by position; (2) content clipped to nothing in a state at 1440 (a collapsed accordion panel, the other slides of a carousel) counts as not displayed only if another declared state of the same screen and language shows it at 1440 (the accordion's expanded state, each slide's state), and that state is then graded normally; (3) content clipped in every declared state (a fixed-height card whose last paragraph lies below the edge), or partly clipped anywhere (a partly cut accordion, a marquee), fails. A read-more clamp is the one partial cut that can pass: intentional truncation (`line-clamp`, or `text-overflow: ellipsis` on the cut edge) passes only when a declared state shows the full text at that width, and fails otherwise; a fixed-height box that cuts text without a clamp fails even with an expanded state. Reveals and truncations are matched to the revealing state by text, not by DOM position. Designers must declare reveal states: every expanded panel, slide or other content hidden by a box that cannot scroll needs a declared state, with its own page, that shows it, or the handoff is blocked. In the wireframe phase, where only default states are rendered, an unresolved reveal is DEFERRED. `evidence.json` records each clip with its `rule` (`reveal`, `truncated`, `partly clipped`, `shown at 1440, lost at this width` or `clip`), the cut elements (`whole`, `partial`) and the state that reveals them (`revealedBy`), each width's `lost` text with the state where it is reachable (`reachableIn`), and media crops (`mediaCrops`, information only).
    24	At the 320 px reflow width, ordinary text or media overflowing any sideways scroller, to the left or the right, fails. A `table`, `pre`, `code`, `role="grid"`/`role="table"` with real rows, or an element marked `data-fannan-2d="reason"` is only a **candidate**, recorded in `evidence.json` with its element and reason and never excused automatically: its scroller must fit inside 320 px and be keyboard-usable, the text around it must reflow, resizable media is not a candidate, and every candidate needs a `reflowExceptions` decision in the handoff, or the final handoff is blocked. There is no cap on the number of candidates.
    25	
    26	The accent must be the approved palette token (`colors.accent`, else `colors.primary`, from the hash-checked tokens). At final handoff an invalid or missing accent variable, or one that differs from the approved token, fails with a message naming the variable; the wireframe phase records the accent as DEFERRED, never PASS. The accent area is a DOM background-area proxy and is approximate; the screenshot review decides. The automated check does not measure accent area or coverage exactly and does not fail other off-palette colours painted on the page: accent area, coverage and stray colours are decided by the screenshots and the manual `accent-area` and `palette` reviews (panel A; R12 M19 closed on that basis). The accent does not have to appear on a control.
    27	
    28	The spacing-scale probe checks all four margins (logical margins included; `auto` and 0 are ignored), padding and gaps. An element marked `data-fannan-spacing-exception="reason"` is recorded with its element, id, reason, values and screen/state/lang in the handoff evidence (`checks.spacingExceptions`). At final handoff it excuses its values only when the handoff has a matching `spacingExceptions` entry for that element id and screen/state/lang, with a reason and the approver as reviewer; otherwise the handoff is blocked (wireframes record it as DEFERRED). A blank reason, or an element without an id, excuses nothing. Margins on `html` and `body` are screened too.
    29	
    30	Negative spacing **tokens** are banned: the approved scale holds only positive lengths, so a negative value is always off-scale. An approved margin exception (above, with the approver named) may still excuse a negative margin on one element; that is a reviewed per-element decision, not a token (R13 D1 closed on this basis).
    31	
    32	Frames are never excused: there is no `frameExceptions` entry. axe and the design probes run in the main document only, so any `iframe`, `frame`, `object`, `embed` or `fencedframe` fails the final handoff (DEFERRED in wireframes). Where the design needs embedded third-party content (a map, a video, a booking widget), the handoff uses a **static poster** image in its place plus an **engineering note** in `HANDOFF.md` naming the embed, its source and where the engineer inserts it. In-frame scanning is revisited only once every gate runs inside frames (R13 q1).
    33	
    34	## Pass/fail checklist
    35	
    36	Every row is BLOCK unless explicitly marked WARN. `wireframe` evaluates the structure/copy column; visual-only criteria are recorded as deferred, never called PASS. `handoff` requires both columns and all fourteen manual IDs listed below (`generic-hero` on marketing and landing screens only).
    37	
    38	| Rule | Pass mark | Evidence and detector | Phase |
    39	|---|---|---|---|
    40	| Point of view | One coherent idea tied to an audience, job and goal; all three cited | `point-of-view`: brief lines, rationale and annotated recommended direction | Both |
    41	| Section and decision trace | 100% of sections, layout breaks, token choices and expressive media name the brief line and visitor job served | `brief-trace`: section/decision ledger matched to DOM IDs and screenshot | Both; token trace after styling |
    42	| Centering | At most one centered principal section per page | DOM computed text alignment plus composition screenshot; reviewer catches nested/unmarked groups | Both |
    43	| Accent area | At most 10% full-page and at most 10% reviewed viewport | Declared accent token(s), computed-style proxy, screenshot measurement including accent inside media/effects | Visual |
    44	| Type sizes | At most six distinct rendered sizes per page/locale, all mapped to named type tokens | Token list and DOM computed `font-size`; inspect pseudo-elements/SVG/canvas text manually | Visual; structural hierarchy in grayscale |
    45	| Centered icon trio | Zero rows of three centered icon-first feature blocks | DOM grouping and screenshot; changing tags or using SVG does not exempt it | Both |
    46	| Generic hero | Zero vague transferable headlines, including a vague headline above three icon cards, on `marketing` and `landing` screens | `generic-hero`: writer/critic swap test on promotional hero copy and claims of distinction, with the actual hero screenshot; a headline still valid for unrelated firms fails. Not required on `functional` screens, which use clear task headings | Both |
    47	| Default effects | Zero generic gradient heroes and zero stock glassmorphism | Background-image/backdrop-filter detector (computed on `html`, `body`, visible elements and rendered `::before`/`::after`, inside open shadow roots, plus constructed stylesheets and the raw text of every served CSS/HTML/SVG/JS file, style attributes and `<style>` elements, so the `-webkit-` alias and root-element filters that Chrome drops still fail; `data:`/`blob:` stylesheets are refused and unreadable unserved ones fail; same-origin frames are only partially inspected, for glass and width, and every `iframe`, `frame`, `object`, `embed` or `fencedframe` fails the final handoff) plus screenshot; decorative effects cannot be relabelled data visuals | Both in grayscale; visual final |
    48	| Rounded-card grid | Zero repeated default rounded-card grids | `card-grid`: actual grouped content, borders/radii and screenshot; use the content's appropriate structure | Both |
    49	| Purple-blue default | Zero default purple-blue decorative pairing | Color-role inventory and screenshot; semantic status color is reviewed for its job, never used as a second decorative accent | Visual |
    50	| Decoration | Every non-content mark has a named comprehension, navigation, hierarchy or identity job | `decoration-job`: asset/element ID → brief line → purpose; unsupported marks are removed | Both |
    51	| Dashboard tropes | Zero undifferentiated KPI-card rows, icon soup, color-only status, fake live tickers | `dashboard-tropes`: task-flow review, screenshots, data/source/timestamp labels and state checks | Both |
    52	| Copy provenance | Every slot's displayed text appears in the brief or writer's deck; every claim maps to a checked, confirmed fact | Slot source lines + source revision/hash; writer checks names/location/partners against project rules | Both |
    53	| Copy filler | Zero occurrences of `unlock`, `seamless`, `empower`, `cutting-edge` and their listed variants in visible copy | English word-boundary and normalized Arabic cliché scan; critic read-through in every required language | Both |
    54	| Rule-of-three tagline | Zero empty three-part slogans | Headline-only automated punctuation/conjunction screening plus mandatory `rule-of-three` semantic review; useful three-item task/data lists are not slogans | Both |
    55	| Stats/quotes/logos | Zero unconfirmed or unlicensed proof claims | Fact ledger, exact quotation/source, permission and asset manifest; numeric regex is only a warning detector for this broader review | Both |
    56	| Placeholders | All visible as `[placeholder]` with IDs during draft; zero at final handoff | Copy deck + rendered DOM + placeholder inventory; scan lorem ipsum, TBD and hidden substitutes | Draft recorded; final BLOCK |
    57	| Craft | Consistent named type/spacing/grid/color roles and purposeful hierarchy | `craft`: token sheet, alignment overlay, actual content and comparative reference critique | Structural before style; complete at handoff |
    58	| Real reference comparison | Three inspected references with dated notes and project-specific lessons | `reference-work`: source URLs, captures/notes, what to adopt and reject, brief trace | Both |
    59	| Complete accessibility evidence | No failed applicable WCAG 2.2 A/AA criterion or unresolved automated incomplete result (a colour-contrast incomplete result resolves only as set out under Automated contrast incomplete results) | `wcag-review`: axe (tags `wcag2a`, `wcag2aa`, `wcag21a`, `wcag21aa`, `wcag22a`, `wcag22aa` from `tools/axe-tags.mjs`, plus `target-size`; axe's experimental A/AA rules stay off and are manual) + keyboard/screen-reader/manual checklist + reference-state captures | Both at design level; full proof at handoff |
    60	| All states/locales and copy fit | Every requested variant accounted for; no clipping, truncation or meaningless N/A | Component matrix, real translations, 150% synthetic stress evidence, LTR/RTL review | Both at current fidelity |
    61	| Observational improvement | Optional non-blocking preference with owner and disposition | WARN only when no contract, accessibility, truth, approval or implementation question is affected | Both |
    62	
    63	Six sizes serve six semantic tiers without making every label another style. Ten percent makes “about ten percent” a reproducible upper bound. The rules are intentionally strict constraints requested for this bot; they do not establish universal rules of good design.
    64	
    65	## Manual evidence that the checker requires
    66	
    67	`manualChecks` includes `point-of-view`, `brief-trace`, `generic-hero` (marketing and landing screens only), `decoration-job`, `card-grid`, `dashboard-tropes`, `rule-of-three`, `reference-work`, `craft`, `wcag-review`, `accent-area`, `palette`, `rules-copy`, `language-overflow`, `discovery-evidence` (the Step 1 discovery ledger), `navigation-findability` (Step 3 navigation model and its Step 5 findability test), `interaction-states` (reachable states beyond the seven), `form-behavior` (forms and consequential actions, or a justified N/A in the note), `task-test` (Step 5 neutral task testing and retests), `feedback-log` (the approver's feedback log), `emphasis-order` (the emphasis order and blurred-screenshot check), `type-measure` (the type scale with measure, line height and the SC 1.4.12 text-spacing check), `colour-roles` (the colour roles and token-level contrast matrix) `grid-per-width` (the grid for every declared desktop width) and `component-consistency` (each reused component matches its one shared definition on every screen; judged structurally in wireframes and fully at handoff). The `craft` artifact may hold the last four; each still needs its own record. In the wireframe phase `accent-area` and `palette` are DEFERRED, and `emphasis-order`, `type-measure`, `colour-roles` and `grid-per-width` are PASS or DEFERRED; every other ID needs PASS in both phases.
    68	
    69	The feedback-log artifact records each item's exact words, scope, version, interpretation, decision, resulting change and confirmation. A deferral needs the approver's recorded disposition. Missing or unresolved items fail. If no feedback was received, record that explicitly with reviewer and scope. Every record identifies its screen, language and state, PASS/FAIL, reviewer, artifact path, brief line references and a concrete note. An artifact may cover several states, but each record identifies the part supporting its exact scope. The artifact contains the actual rubric/check result, screenshot references and measured values where relevant. Missing coverage for a screen is a failure even if another screen passed. A grayscale artifact records visual tests as deferred while judging structural craft; it does not sign off final color or typography.
    70	
    71	On every screen type (the `copy-truth` row; on `marketing` and `landing` screens also `generic-hero`), the critic asks: with the company name masked, does the headline still express a precise offering, audience task or independently sourced distinction from this brief? Record the compared alternative categories and the reason. If it could be pasted unchanged onto arbitrary company sites, fail and return the slot to the writer. Do not replace an approved headline without the existing approval process.
    72	
    73	For functional dashboards, derive content from the user's decisions and workflows. Use a ranked queue, trend or table when that is what the task requires; do not add a summary-card row merely because dashboards often have one. Every number requires a real source, scope and update meaning. Static sample data must be explicitly labelled as a fixture and cannot enter a real handoff as production data. “Live” requires a real documented data path; animation is not evidence.
    74	
    75	## Accessibility evidence, accurately scoped
    76	
    77	Record each text foreground/background pair and state. Normal text needs 4.5:1 contrast; large text needs 3:1 using WCAG's definition of large text. Contrast uses specified/computed colors, not antialiased screenshot edges. Do not round a failing ratio upward. [W3C contrast guidance](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/contrast-minimum.html).
    78	
    79	Applicable control boundaries, meaningful icons and graphical objects need 3:1 against adjacent colors. Record their actual role and applicable exceptions rather than claiming every decorative border must pass. Status also has a readable label or shape. [W3C non-text contrast](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/non-text-contrast.html).
    80	
    81	Record sequential keyboard focus, visible focus before/after, modal entry/escape/return, traps and sticky overlays. AA focus-not-obscured requires that the focused control is not entirely hidden by author content; prefer full visibility as this project's stricter usability target. Focus Appearance 2.4.13 is AAA, so do not mislabel it as the AA criterion. [W3C focus-not-obscured](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/focus-not-obscured-minimum).
    82	
    83	Measure actual pointer targets. A 24 × 24 CSS-pixel target is the baseline; any smaller target needs a documented qualifying spacing, equivalent-control, inline, user-agent or essential exception. Bounding boxes and an “inline” tag are only screening heuristics. [W3C target-size guidance](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/target-size-minimum).
    84	
    85	Test non-dragging alternatives for dragging operations, and accessible authentication without an unsupported cognitive test where authentication exists. If absent, record N/A with the screen inventory that proves absence. [W3C WCAG 2.2 additions](https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/new-in-22/).
    86	
    87	Check 200% text resizing and 400% desktop zoom/reflow (equivalent 320 CSS-pixel width), including applicable two-dimensional content exceptions. This is accessibility validation, not authorization to design phone layouts. Document browser zoom and viewport used. [W3C reflow guidance](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/reflow.html).
    88	
    89	Set the page language, mark language changes and specify base direction separately. Test real RTL content, mixed-direction identifiers, punctuation and numbers; use appropriate directional markup rather than reversing strings. [W3C internationalization guidance](https://www.w3.org/International/questions/qa-html-dir).
    90	
    91	## Command and release decision
    92	
    93	```sh
    94	FANNAN_DEPS=/opt/fannan/deps \
    95	node "$FANNAN_RELEASE/tools/pre-handoff-check.mjs" \
    96	  /workspace/design-output/PROJECT/DESIGN.md \
    97	  /workspace/design-output/PROJECT/reference.html \
    98	  /workspace/design-output/PROJECT/evidence-vNN \
    99	  PROJECT /workspace/design-output/PROJECT/handoff.json --phase=handoff
   100	```
   101	
   102	Use `--phase=wireframe` for the complete wireframe quality gate, run within the existing Step 5 gate after the task and navigation-findability tests and necessary retests; it must PASS before structure approval and Step 6. Replace `PROJECT` with the validated project slug and use a fresh output directory. A nonzero result blocks. A missing protected checker binary, dependency, reference, approval or manual evidence remains a failure; never replace it with a stub in a production run. The separate test suite may stub a boundary only when its output is clearly labelled test-only and cannot masquerade as release evidence.
     1	# Step 9 package contract
     2	
     3	`handoff.json` is the authored source of truth. `HANDOFF.md` is its readable checklist, generated by the checker. Keep one set of criterion IDs; do not maintain a second design/QA acceptance list. Freeze the source JSON, reference frames and all linked evidence by version/hash. Any affected change requires fresh checks and approval. Paths in the JSON are resolved from the command's working directory; use absolute paths for release evidence to avoid ambiguity.
     4	
     5	This contract specifies what each real project must supply. The included synthetic fixture is only an executable checker example. It is not a real project's copy, factual proof, approval, licensed asset set or completed handoff.
     6	
     7	## Fixed package contents
     8	
     9	| Artifact | Required contents and exact acceptance |
    10	|---|---|
    11	| `handoff.json` and generated `HANDOFF.md` | Version 1 record; project/phase; no open questions; no placeholders; all screens, locales and states; one criterion ID per check; actual evidence and owner/reviewer |
    12	| Source snapshot | Project settings, rules, brief, fact register and copy-deck revisions; source hashes and cited lines; current step and permitted scope |
    13	| Private design link | `designLink.url`, `private:true` and evidence of access/privacy; tool and frame IDs/version; no public upload as a shortcut |
    14	| `DESIGN.md` | Reviewed narrative and token frontmatter; Google format lint with zero errors; token decisions traced to brief lines |
    15	| Tokens | DTCG-style JSON containing type, spacing, colors and breakpoints; generated CSS variables; executable build script, actual config and locked dependency versions; no hand-edited generated CSS |
    16	| Screen/state inventory | Stable screen and component IDs; default, hover, focus, disabled, empty, loading and error all accounted for, plus every additional reachable state and form behavior below; N/A requires reason, reviewer and evidence; applicable states specify appearance, behavior and recovery |
    17	| Reference frames | 1440-pixel-wide PNGs; `screen--state--locale--vNN.png`; actual file hash and version; every applicable state/language; reference implementation HTML when used |
    18	| Final copy deck | Per-slot ID, screen, purpose, kind, exact final text, min/max character range, tone, language/direction, source lines, claim IDs and placement screenshot; variants use actual approved translations |
    19	| Assets | File, source URL/document, creator, licence/permission, consent record where needed, intended use and crop; font family/weight files and licence; file size and project's budget result |
    20	| Motion | Job, duration in ms, easing, trigger, affected element, interruption/cleanup, static reduced-motion fallback, no-JS and script-failure behavior |
    21	| Acceptance | Per-screen criteria with ID, BLOCK/WARN, observable pass/fail check, decision and evidence. Failed BLOCK stops. WARN needs impact, owner and approved disposition with no unresolved question |
    22	| Accessibility | WCAG 2.2 AA, contrast-pair table, focus order and captures, target sizes/exceptions, alt text and intent, heading outline, standalone action labels, manual audit and automated incomplete-result resolution |
    23	| Audit intent | One H1 per page; complete semantic outline; intent for every image's alt text; independent link/button labels; page purpose, search intent, proposed title/description, index/canonical decision, schema type and source-backed properties or an explicit reason for none |
    24	| Approval and critic | Final scoped approval with date, approver and every frame ID/hash; the approver's feedback log with every item resolved or explicitly deferred (plan, "Folding in the approver's feedback"), recorded as the `feedback-log` manual check; independent critic rounds, reference work, row scores and PASS/FAIL; no open P0/P1 |
    25	| Tool evidence | Runtime/tool versions, exact commands, stdout/exit, machine JSON and screenshots; declared test-only evidence excluded from actual approval |
    26	
    27	Tokens include the desktop reference width and documented layout breakpoints needed for the authorized scope. They do not invent mobile/tablet styling while those phases are deferred. Breakpoint CSS variables are a handoff representation; standard CSS custom properties cannot be substituted directly into media-query conditions. The engineer translates those values into the project's existing build conventions.
    28	
    29	## JSON fields
    30	
    31	`tools/handoff-contract.mjs` is the executable validator. Use its field names exactly; do not infer an unvalidated schema from prose. The following is its authoring guide:
    32	
    33	- `version`, `phase`, `brief.path`, `rules.path`, and `settings` identify the contract and inputs. Settings provides `path`, `repository`, `stack` and `roles.engineer`, `roles.qaGate`, `roles.writer`, `roles.approver`, matching the actual project settings. Fannan copies those values; it never selects a stack. `settings.desktopWidths` lists the project's declared desktop CSS widths: unique positive integers that always include the canonical 1440 (for example `[1440]` or `[1440, 1280]`); a missing, duplicate or 1440-less list fails the contract in both phases (the wireframe is a desktop handoff too; there is no default list). It declares widths to check; it does not authorize a mobile phase.
    34	- `pointOfView.idea` and `pointOfView.briefRefs` establish the visual thesis. A separate decision/section ledger links every section, grid break, token and expressive element to source lines and the relevant audience task.
    35	- `languages` lists each `{lang,dir}`. Every screen has its page, a `type` (`marketing`, `landing` or `functional`: promotional pages are `marketing` or `landing`, task screens such as settings, dashboards and forms are `functional`; the `generic-hero` manual check is required only for `marketing` and `landing` screens; `functional` is for task screens without promotional hero copy, and promotional copy on any screen type is still reviewed by the `copy-truth` row), `headingOutline`, `seoIntent`, `schemaIntent`, `images` with alt text/intent, and `actions` with standalone labels. Empty image/action arrays must match the real page; omission is not evidence of absence.
    36	- `screens[].states` accounts for the seven state names **per language**, with `lang` and `dir`. Applicable states have `page` and `frame:{path,width:1440,version,sha256}`. A non-applicable state has `applicable:false`, `reason`, `reviewer` and `evidence`; default is always applicable. A page-level state may show the relevant component in that state; do not invent a global error screen for a static page.
    37	- Shared component inventory (`components`, `component-consistency`): Maintain one canonical specification for each reused component: anatomy, semantic variants, sizes, token bindings, content constraints and composition/usage rules. Map every screen instance to that specification and the existing component where available. Compare equivalent instances across screens; unexplained differences fail. Each entry is `{id, variants, states, tokens, behaviour}` (optional `anatomy`, `sizes`, `usage`, `content`); ids are unique, `states` includes `default`, and `tokens` maps each property to a token (it may be empty in the wireframe phase, never at handoff). Every `screens[].components[]` entry names a shared `id`, may choose a declared `variant`, and accounts for its states, whose applicable names must be in the shared definition; a specification field it repeats must equal the shared definition, so one component specified differently on two screens fails. The critic's final round records `componentComparison: {screens, decision, evidence}` covering every screen.
    38	- `screens[].components[].states` also accounts for each state, including justified N/A. Attach appearance, behavior, entry/exit triggers, keyboard behavior and recovery in the component specification referenced by the inventory. Every non-default component state must be represented by a real reference frame or state sheet.
    39	- Reachable states beyond the seven (component specification and `interaction-states`): The seven baseline states are a minimum. Derive additional reachable states from actual interactions, including success/completion, selected/checked, expanded/collapsed, validation and read-only where applicable. Specify triggers, appearance, approved copy, semantics, announcements, keyboard/focus behavior and reference evidence. Success communicates the confirmed outcome and next action. The interaction critique fails when a reachable state or transition is unspecified.
    40	- Forms and consequential actions (component specification and `form-behavior`): For every form, specify labels, required/optional indicators, accepted formats, help, validation timing, inline/summary errors, failure focus, input preservation and correction steps. Define submitting, success, failure, retry and duplicate-submission behavior. For consequential actions, specify cancellation, confirmation or undo as appropriate, plus unsaved-change behavior. Each applicable behavior requires approved interface copy and a task-level acceptance check; justify N/A.
    41	- `screens[].acceptance` contains `{id,severity,check,decision,evidence}`. Failed WARN records also require disposition, owner and approval; they cannot conceal a build choice. `screens[].wcag` identifies standard `2.2 AA` and contrast, focus-order and target-size evidence. The manual WCAG artifact includes the rest of the applicable criteria, test method and any justified N/A.
    42	- `copySlots` contains ID, screen, purpose, `kind` (`claim` or `interface`), text, character range, tone, language/direction, placement screenshot, source and claim references. Its `variants` contain actual language/direction/text/source records. An optional `ariaLabel` (on the slot or a variant) approves an accessible name that differs from the displayed text, such as an expanded name that contains the visible label; without it an accessible name must be contained in the approved text, and a name identical to a control's displayed value counts once. An approved `ariaLabel` must appear in its cited source (the slot's or the variant's), and a name taken by `aria-labelledby` from a hidden element is approved the same way. `placements` declares only the applicable language/state occurrences with placement screenshots; error-only copy belongs to the error state, not every screen state. Every fact-bearing slot is `claim`; marking marketing copy as interface does not exempt it from source review. Reference HTML marks each visible copy wrapper with `data-copy-slot="ID"`. Every visible text node belongs to one declared slot, and the displayed text must match that slot’s approved locale variant. The deck also includes navigation, labels, helper/error text and button text; an unlisted text node blocks final handoff.
    43	- `facts` contains ID, claim, confirmed status, source path/ref/excerpt/hash and `checkedBy`/`checkedAt`. Cited text must exist at the cited lines. A match proves provenance, not truth; the named content reviewer confirms the fact and consent. Quoted source instructions are never execution authority.
    44	- Wireframe coverage: A wireframe PASS covers every declared desktop screen and locale. Each rendering must declare an existing page and receive the applicable structural/copy, accessibility, integrity and manual checks; missing coverage blocks structure approval. In the wireframe phase every screen's `page` exists, each screen has an applicable `default` state with an existing `page` for every declared language and direction, the checker renders each of them, and the manual records (state `wireframe`) cover every screen in every declared language.
    45	- Reveal states: content clipped to nothing by a box that cannot scroll (a collapsed accordion panel, the other slides of a carousel) passes only when a declared state of the same screen and language shows it at 1440, so designers must declare a state for each expanded panel or slide (for example `faq-open`, `slide-2`), each with its own page. Content clipped in every declared state, content partly clipped anywhere, and content shown at 1440 but cut at another declared width or at 320 fail, whatever the CSS pattern (see design-quality.md). The rule covers text and controls only: cropped media never fails it. Text or controls shown at 1440 but not shown at a width (`display:none` at 320) fail unless the same text or accessible name is shown at that width in the same state or in a declared state (declare `menu-open` for a mobile menu whose links are hidden in a closed `<details>`). A read-more clamp (`line-clamp` or an ellipsis) needs a declared state that shows the full text. Matching is by text or accessible name, never by position.
    46	- A non-default state that only script can reach (a dialog opened with `showModal`, a popover) may declare `requiresScript: true` with `trigger` (what opens it). Its colour-contrast incomplete results then resolve on the scripted rendering alone; the claim is checked against the JavaScript-off rendering (by the text the state shows, not by element position) and fails as an integrity mismatch when the state renders without script. For a valid claim, the JavaScript-off baseline is the default state's JavaScript-off rendering (same screen and language): content only the scripted state shows passes only as approved copy slots whose `placements` include this state and language (the slot's text and the headings and controls inside it); any other added content fails the content comparison. The state may replace a default slot only when the copy deck places the new slot in it and does not place the old one there; changes to shared copy fail. The default state cannot declare it.
    47	- `manualChecks` records criterion ID, screen, language, state, decision, reviewer, artifact, note and brief refs. A PASS `wcag-review` record whose artifact is the state's frame or screenshot (or whose note names it) is what resolves an automated colour-contrast incomplete result that the visibility backstop measured uncovered, with its faintest text (the 10th percentile of glyph clusters against the local background) at AA, in both renderings. Each applicable screen/state/language receives every required criterion. A contradictory or duplicate verdict is not a pass. Use the IDs in `runtime/quality-checks.json`.
    48	- `critic.referenceWork` points to the inspected board; `critic.rounds` records up to three rounds, evidence, reviewer, copy checks and design checks. Each check names its critic-rubric row in `row` (`brief-point-of-view`, `copy-truth`, `hierarchy-task-flow`, `typography-language`, `spacing-rhythm-grid`, `colour-imagery-restraint`, `interaction-states-motion` or `accessibility-handoff`), and the final round needs at least one passing, evidenced check per row. The underlying artifact includes the full eight-row rubric, scores and improvement totals. Final checks must all pass; see the critic rubric for the early-stop rule.
    49	- Review records are scoped. Each entry names `screen`, `state` and `lang` of a declared, applicable state and applies to that rendering only; one entry per element (or check) and scope.
    50	  - Optional `reflowExceptions` records the decision for each 2D reflow candidate the 320 px screen reports: `{id, screen, state, lang, reason, reviewer}`, where `id` is the candidate element's id and `reviewer` is the qaGate or approver. An unresolved candidate blocks the final handoff, as does an id that names different elements in one scope.
    51	  - Optional `spacingExceptions` backs each `data-fannan-spacing-exception` on the page: `{id, screen, state, lang, reason, reviewer}`, where `reviewer` is the approver. An exception without a matching entry blocks the final handoff.
    52	  - Optional `jsOffDifferences` records an expected JavaScript-off difference: `{check, screen, state, lang, reason, reviewer}`, where `check` is one integrity field (`glass`, `h1`, `placeholders`, `centered`, `typeSizes`, `heroGradient`, `purpleBlue`, `iconRows`, `accent`, `accentRatio`, `maxViewportAccent`, `filler`, `ruleOfThree`, `frames`, `stylesheetsUnscanned`, `spacingOffScale`, `horizontalOverflow`, `clipped`, `offscreenLeft`, `unreachable`, `scrolledText`, `unmeasured`, `unscannedScopes`, `reflowFail`, `contrastFailures`, `contrastUnknown`, `contrastChecked`, `contrastEffects`, `textMeasured`, `textObscured`, `textUnreadable`, `textUnmeasured`, `textHidden` or `content`) and `reviewer` is the qaGate or approver. A reason without a reviewer excuses nothing; without a matching entry the difference fails as an integrity mismatch. Different JavaScript-off content (copy slots and headings compared with their text) is never PASS: unexplained it is a mismatch, and with a reviewed `content` entry the entry is recorded but the cross-check stays INCOMPLETE, because the JavaScript-off metrics then prove nothing (R13 N14). Zero JavaScript-off contrast coverage, where the scripted page had coverage, is INCOMPLETE too. The rendered-visibility backstop (`references/design-quality.md`) adds `textMeasured`, `textObscured`, `textUnreadable`, `textUnmeasured` and `textHidden` per rendering: text obscured or unreadable with JavaScript on fails, text lost with JavaScript off (including more text clipped to nothing) is a mismatch that a reviewed entry records but never makes PASS, and unmeasurable text is INCOMPLETE. Text clipped to nothing by an ancestor that cannot be scrolled, the same in both renderings, is not displayed (`textHidden`) and is graded through the declared states. Pixels decide visibility; AA stays with the CSS pass whenever it resolves the colours. Unresolved compositing on displayed text in either rendering blocks handoff as INCOMPLETE. Token contrast proofs and `jsOffDifferences` cannot waive it. Retaining the effect requires a reviewed measurement implementation that resolves its actual composition for every applicable rendering. Backdrop-filter remains a hard FAIL. The cross-check runs on the positional page and on every declared state; the main gates still fail scripted glass and the other hard fails.
    53	- `designLink`, `tokens`, `assets`, `motion`, `toolEvidence`, `approval`, `openQuestions` and `placeholders` complete the package. Each of `tokens.json`, `tokens.css`, `tokens.buildScript`, `tokens.config` and `tokens.lock` has `{path,sha256}`. `tokens.buildEvidence.path` points to the `.build.json` receipt written by the reviewed Style Dictionary build, binding input, output, script, config and lock hashes. The checker never executes an arbitrary project-supplied script. Approval names its record, decision, approver and `frameIds` in the form `screen/lang/state/version/sha256`; the dated approval artifact repeats those exact IDs.
    54	
    55	Character counts are not permission to shorten approved content. For a multilingual slot use language-specific ranges when necessary, revise the contract and revalidate. Rendering, grapheme clusters and bidirectional order require visual review even if a string-length check passes.
    56	
    57	## Evidence and responsibilities
    58	
    59	The writer owns exact copy, fact confirmation and wording review. Fannan owns design decisions and the package. The critic reviews independently. The approver owns the human decision. The project's engineer confirms that the package leaves no build question and implements only in the repository/stack from settings. The QA gate reuses the same criterion IDs against the implementation and records preview URL, commit SHA, build output, screenshots, test steps and actual result. Fannan's prototype evidence cannot stand in for that implementation QA.
    60	
    61	The generated checklist is a review surface, not an authorization token. Compare its source JSON hash and artifact versions before relying on it. A full PASS requires all checks from the current machine gate plus verified manual artifacts and actual approval. A manually typed `PASS`, a copied report or a valid hash of fabricated evidence is insufficient; inspect provenance and authorship during review.
    62	
    63	## Missing inputs and change control
    64	
    65	At draft phases, keep missing text visibly marked and register its owner. At Step 9, even one unresolved question or placeholder blocks. Missing licences, references, claims, tool evidence, role mappings or approval are treated the same way. The issue list states the exact item, owner, next action and impact. The task remains Stuck/On hold; there is no partial handoff labelled complete.
    66	
    67	After final approval, a change to source copy, tokens, state behavior, frame or acceptance requirement invalidates affected checks and approval. Create a fresh version and output directory; never overwrite evidence already cited by approval. Reuse unaffected evidence only when its dependency hashes and scope still match.

