| R22-GPT-01 | A | P2 | `release/tools/visibility.mjs:455,462–479` | **Readable opacity captions become INCOMPLETE.** The cutoff is `coreAlpha: 0.75`, followed by `if (a < opts.coreAlpha) continue`. Black/white capture differences include composited CSS opacity. Ordinary white photo-caption text at `opacity: .7` therefore has no eligible core pixels even where contrast comfortably passes AA. Executed four-shot pixel reproduction: contrast **9.90:1**, `glyphRatio.ratio: null`; `resolveContrastIncomplete` remains blocked despite a citing PASS review. | Separate geometric glyph coverage from text/ancestor opacity, then grade the actual composited painted colour. Preserve fail-closed behavior for unresolved composition. Add browser regressions for passing 70%-opacity text over a dark photograph, opacity on its ancestor, and genuinely failing translucent text. |
| R22-GPT-02 | A | P2 | `release/tools/visibility.mjs:473–478` | **The “punctuation” exemption also drops failing letters.** The actual rule is `px.length < opts.punctPixels && solid`; it does not identify punctuation or preserve low-ratio clusters. Small ordinary letters such as `i`/`l` can have fewer than 12 core pixels. Executed synthetic reproduction with three nine-pixel letter stems over a near-white patch and a larger letter over dark background: result **15.91:1**, three clusters dropped; retaining those cores gives **1.23:1**. Specific real-font rendering is UNVERIFIED. The existing small-grey fixture has passing contrast throughout and cannot catch this omission. | Restrict punctuation exclusions using correspondence to the source text, rather than area alone. Retain confidently measured failing letter cores; uncertain coverage stays INCOMPLETE. Add thin-letter light-patch failures alongside passing small-text and punctuation regressions. |
| R22-GPT-03 | A | P2 | `release/tools/width-screen.mjs:84,97–100` | **Standard media fallback text defeats the crop exemption.** `kindOf` tests `ownText(d)` before `d.matches(MEDIA)`. Thus `<video> Your browser does not support this video. </video>` becomes `kind: 'text'`, although `contentRects` measures its video box. An executed DOM-model reproduction of a 300px video cropped by a 200px frame reports a partial text clip. This is ordinary fallback markup. The same classifier affects canvas fallback text. Existing video fixtures omit fallback text. | Classify native media before inspecting their own fallback text. Continue checking separately rendered text/control descendants. Add positive cropped-video and cropped-canvas regressions containing standard fallback content, retaining negative caption/control cases. |
| R22-GPT-04 | A | P2 | `release/tools/width-screen.mjs:19,50,83–89` | **Named icon links are absent from both cut and loss inventories.** `isContent` accepts only `MEDIA` or `ownText`; `CONTROL` excludes links and other semantic controls. An ordinary `<a href="/profile" aria-label="Your profile"><svg>…</svg></a>` contributes only excluded media. Executed DOM-model width reproduction: hiding that link at 320 produces empty loss lists and **PASS**. Cropping its interactive box likewise lacks a control cut to measure. | Include links and supported semantic controls independently of painted text, using their interactive boxes and accessible names. Preserve the image-only crop exemption inside an otherwise fully available link. Add failures for a disappearing/cropped named icon link and a positive image-zoom link case. |
| R22-GPT-05 | A | P2 | `release/tools/width-screen.mjs:86–87,201,255–258` | **Plain text can substitute for lost functionality, and distinct controls collapse together.** `printOf` returns only the string, without kind/role; `nameOf` omits `aria-labelledby`. Executed DOM-model reproductions both **PASS**: a Subscribe button disappears while a static “Subscribe” heading remains; one of two distinctly `aria-labelledby`-named checkboxes disappears while both labels remain. The latter controls both print as `control:input`. These are ordinary responsive form mistakes. | Match text and controls in separate semantic namespaces. Control keys must include role/type and the resolved accessible name, including `aria-labelledby` and supported native naming. Reuse the existing naming logic where possible. Add both negative reproductions and retain passing menu relocation/name-preserving icon-button tests. |
| R22-GPT-06 | A | P2 | `release/tools/width-screen.mjs:256–258,282–285` | **A clamp can permanently lose whole paragraphs.** Any partial cut classifies the box as `truncated`, but resolution uses only `c.partial`: `c.rule === 'reveal' ? c.whole : c.partial`. Meanwhile, `lostByClip` suppresses both lists from independent loss reporting. Executed resolver reproduction: a clamp partly cuts paragraph one and wholly hides paragraph two; its expanded state shows only paragraph one. Both widths and the overall resolver return **PASS**, with no unresolved items. Multi-paragraph read-more excerpts are ordinary. | Resolve every wholly and partly cut content item in a truncation at the same width. Do not permit truncation styling to excuse clipped controls. Add a multi-paragraph expanded-state omission failure, its complete-content passing counterpart, and a clipped-control negative case. |
| R22-GPT-07 | B | P3 | `release/references/handoff-contract.md:47`; `release/tests/designer-process.test.mjs:199` | The contract still specifies “the 10th percentile of glyph clusters against the local background,” contradicting the minimum measure at `design-quality.md:19`. The retirement assertion checks only design-quality, so the documentation suite passes with this contradiction. | Replace that parenthetical with: **“the minimum of the median recovered contrast ratios of glyph-core clusters, using the coverage and small-cluster rules in design-quality.md.”** Extend the regression to reject percentile-based acceptance wording in the handoff contract. |
| R22-GPT-08 | B | P3 | `release/FANNAN-PLAN.md:41`; `release/references/design-quality.md:60`; `release/references/handoff-contract.md:45` | “Do not cut copy, ellipsize content,” “no clipping, truncation,” and “content partly clipped anywhere … fail” conflict with the newly authorized read-more exception at `design-quality.md:23` and later in contract `:45`. | At plan `:41`, add: **“An intentional read-more clamp may show an excerpt only when a declared state makes the complete approved text reachable at every checked width; all other cutting or ellipsis fails.”** At quality `:60`, replace the blanket prohibition with **“no unrecoverable clipping or truncation; read-more clamps follow the declared-state rule above.”** Qualify the contract’s partial-cut prohibition with **“except an intentional read-more clamp meeting the full-text-state rule below.”** |

